'''''Survivor 49''''' is the forty-ninth season of the CBS competitive reality television series ''{{dab|Survivor|U.S.}}''.

''Survivor 49'' began with a collapse of the [[Kele|Kele Tribe]], losing four members in a row. After a tribe swap into two tribes, the first season in the New Era to do so, The old [[Uli]] members came together on new [[Hina]] with [[Sophi Balerdi]] to take out old Hina. [[Uli Alliance|This alliance]] spent the early merge struggling for power against the Hina alliance, and eventually the Tres Leches alliance, consisting of [[Savannah Louie]], [[Rizo Velovic]], and Sophi Balerdi, emerged victorious as they made the final four together utilizing Rizo's [[Hidden Immunity Idol]] and Savannah's ability to win [[Immunity Challenge|Immunity Challenges]]. After 26 days, Savannah defeated Sophi and [[Sage Ahrens-Nichols]] in a 5-2-1 vote.

Prior to its airdate, ''Survivor 49'' was announced to have produced two returning players for the upcoming [[Survivor 50: In the Hands of the Fans|50th season]]. Those players were officially revealed to be Rizo Velovic and Savannah Louie after ''Survivor 49'' finished airing, though their names were heavily rumored during airing. To date, there are no other players to return for another season.

==Production==
12 hours before the season's marooning, two would-be contestants were removed from the game for violating the pregame rules during the preseason [[Ponderosa]], specifically with regards to talking to the other contestants. They were aptly replaced by [[Jason Treul]] and [[MC Chukwujekwu]].<ref>https://parade.com/tv/survivor-49-cast-2025</ref><ref>https://ew.com/jeff-probst-explains-survivor-49-players-replaced-alternates-11794636</ref><ref>https://parade.com/tv/jeff-probst-survivor-49-alternates-jason-treul-michelle-mc-chukwujekwu-exclusive</ref><ref>https://ew.com/jeff-probst-players-cut-survivor-49-disrespecting-rules-11816250</ref>

One week prior to the season's air date, a special video was uploaded to the ''SurvivorCBS'' YouTube channel titled "''[[36 Hour Survivor: Influencer Experience]]''". In the video, eight social media influencers spent 36 hours simulating an episode of ''Survivor''. They built a shelter, searched for a [[Hidden Immunity Idol]], competed in an [[Immunity Challenge]], and voted someone out at [[Tribal Council]]. The video also gave insight into some of ''Survivor 49''<nowiki/>'s set designs and props, including a challenge design, the Tribal Council set, and the torch [[snuffer]].
==Twists/Changes==
* '''26-Day Game'''
* '''Tribe Division: '''The 18 castaways were divided into 3 tribes of 6. No unifying theme was used in dividing the tribes.
* '''Penalties for Losing Immunity Challenges'''
*'''[[Shot in the Dark]]'''
*'''[[Summit|Journeys]]''':
**'''[[Advantage Menu|Advantage Option]]:''' The person who won the challenge on the Day 4 journey could choose to take one of two advantages:
***'''[[Vote Steal]]:''' The challenge winner could choose to prevent one of the challenge losers' right to vote at their next Tribal Council. In exchange, they would receive the right to vote twice at a future Tribal Council.
***'''[[Challenge Advantage|Tribe Disadvantage]]:''' The challenge winner could choose to impose a disadvantage on one of the other two tribes at the next Immunity Challenge.
**[[Vote Blocker|'''Vote Blocker''']]
*'''[[Beware Advantage]]s'''
**'''[[Hidden Immunity Idol]]'''
**'''[[Knowledge is Power Advantage|Knowledge is Power]]'''
*'''[[Tribe Switch]]:''' A tribe switch occurred on Day 7, shuffling the remaining fourteen castaways into two tribes of seven, dissolving the {{tribeicon|uli}} tribe.
*'''Tribe Expansion:''' A tribe expansion occurred on Day 11 with the reconstitution of the Uli tribe, spreading the remaining twelve castaways into three tribes of four.
*'''Traditional [[Merge]]:''' For the first time in the New Era, the [[Earn the Merge]] twist was skipped in favor of a traditional merge at the final eleven. However, the players still competed in two teams to earn the merge feast and a chance at Individual Immunity.
*'''Simplified Final Four Tribal Council'''
* '''Reformatted Final Tribal Council:''' Instead of having each jury member speak individually, an open forum was held to ensure a more insightful rapport between the jury and finalists.

